    "done_when": "The review of the-clone-cache-stays-bounded (95) probed reckon/clones.py at c6a2f77b2: a scan of five corpus files stored 5 entries, then a scan of one file stored 1, discarding four warm entries that the next full scan had to re-parse. The cap in clone_matches is _cap_for(len(corpus_paths)), and corpus_paths comes from the caller's head_sources, so a caller passing a subset sets a subset cap. promotion_clone_matches passes the whole tree, so production is not affected today. The cap is derived from the number of distinct corpus paths the cache holds as well as those this scan read, so a partial scan keeps one entry per known file. The bound across revisions still holds: one entry per path, the most recently used. The docstring of _cap_for states the rule and its reason. tests/test_a_partial_scan_keeps_the_whole_cache.py reproduces the probe: a five-file scan, then a one-file scan, then the five-file scan re-parses nothing. It also asserts that scans over more revisions than the cap admits still hold at most one entry per path. The declared negative control is executed and logged. Base and head arms of tests/test_the_clone_cache_stays_bounded.py, tests/test_the_clone_scan_reads_only_the_promoted_diff.py, tests/test_promotion_reports_clones.py and tests/test_velocity_clone_matches.py show zero added failures, each log ending in its pytest summary and EXIT= line.",
